Z||000000||My7Fieros||08-26-2000||04:43 AM||||Ok so Ive just spent 5 hours putting new dew wipes on my Formula...almost as fun as changing the front exhaust manifold!<BR>But man, what a difference it makes! Looks great!||||reg||6
Z||000001||deceler8||08-26-2000||10:40 AM||||I did this the other day and for a change it was a Fiero job that didn't take all day. And I wasn't even swearing at the car afterward.<P>I budgeted an entire day to change both dew strips and an hour later I was done and actually smiling in disbelief. I had no problems with rusted screws, dropped screws, or anything else.<P>Key to doing this job is getting Rodney's dew strip tool...you only need it for the center two screws that are behind the glass. It also helps if you use a little RTV to hold the screws to the tool as you re-install the two screws behind the glass. <P>The mirror can be tricky to remove unless you have a 10MM open end wrench. A socket tends to bind in the small opening through which the mirror bolts are accessed.<P>The new strips really improve the looks of the car. And I got mine at the local Chevy dealer for about $30 each (they were actually in stock).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||08-26-2000||05:06 PM||||I drilled out the window rivits and pulled the glass. While I was in there, it was pretty easy to grease all the window and lock linkages. The replacement rivits are about 50 cents each at a glass shop and it makes the dew wipe job so much easier. But- to each his own. . .||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fearless Freep||08-26-2000||06:28 PM||||I just rebuilt both headlight motors and did the dew strips.  Still had time to mow the lawn, wash dishes, laundry, & cook dinner.  First time I actually didnt have any parts left over!  Rodney's dewtool is the way to go.||||reg||1
